{%- comment -%}
  Catalogue and editorial imagery — port of components/ui/media.tsx.

  Per the design system's product card, an image that does not exist renders as
  a tone-filled block with a small uppercase caption at the bottom — at the exact
  ratio the real photo will occupy, so adding the photography later causes no
  reflow (CLS stays 0). That is the whole reason the catalogue can be previewed
  before the store has a single product in it.

  `ratio` overrides the frame the image is cropped into, which is how the
  design's fixed-height crops are expressed: one source photograph is cropped
  differently by a card, a mosaic tile and a bag thumbnail. `fill` drops the
  frame altogether and takes the size of the nearest positioned ancestor, for the
  bands whose height is set by the layout rather than by the photograph.

  `priority` marks the one image the page is judged on — the product page's main
  shot — and is the only thing that decides how the browser fetches it.

  Accepts: image (Shopify image or nil), sizes, ratio, fill, priority, class, label
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- comment -%}
  FIXED: `loading` and `fetchpriority` were read from `loading_mode` and
  `priority_mode`, and NEITHER WAS EVER ASSIGNED — here or anywhere else in the
  theme. So `image_tag` got nil for both: no image on the site ever carried
  loading="lazy" (an eleven-card grid fetched every photograph eagerly), and the
  product page's LCP image never carried fetchpriority="high" even though its
  caller passes `priority: true` and the header above documents the parameter.
  A silently-ignored parameter is the bug that comes back, so both values now
  derive from `priority` in one place, right here.

  The non-priority case sends the spec's own default, `auto`, rather than a blank
  string: a Liquid filter cannot take a conditional argument, and passing nil
  risks emitting `fetchpriority=""`, which is not a valid value.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- comment -%}
  RULE 3 DEBT, left as-is deliberately: `media_label` is a hardcoded English
  string, and every caller passes a hardcoded English `label:` too — so the
  placeholder's caption and its aria-label are the one user-visible copy in this
  snippet that is not translatable. It wants two interface keys, which only the
  integrator may add: `product.media_placeholder` ("Product photo") for this
  default, and `product.media_angle` ("Angle") for the gallery thumbnails. Not
  invented here.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign media_label = label | default: 'Product photo'

  if priority
    assign loading_mode = 'eager'
    assign priority_mode = 'high'
  else
    assign loading_mode = 'lazy'
    assign priority_mode = 'auto'
  endif

  if fill
    assign box = 'absolute inset-0 h-full w-full'
    assign frame = ''
  else
    assign box = 'w-full'
    if ratio
      assign frame = 'aspect-ratio:' | append: ratio
    elsif image
      assign frame = 'aspect-ratio:' | append: image.width | append: ' / ' | append: image.height
    else
      assign frame = 'aspect-ratio:1 / 1'
    endif
  endif
-%}

{%- if image == blank -%}
  <div
    role="img"
    aria-label="{{ media_label }} placeholder"
    style="{{ frame }}"
    class="flex items-end justify-center rounded-sm bg-tone p-4.5 {{ box }} {{ class }}"
  >
    <span class="text-micro uppercase tracking-nav text-white/75">{{ media_label }}</span>
  </div>
{%- else -%}
  <div style="{{ frame }}" class="relative overflow-hidden rounded-sm bg-tone {{ box }} {{ class }}">
    {{
      image
      | image_url: width: 1200
      | image_tag:
        sizes: sizes,
        widths: '256, 384, 512, 640, 768, 1024, 1200',
        loading: loading_mode,
        fetchpriority: priority_mode,
        class: 'absolute inset-0 h-full w-full object-cover',
        alt: image.alt
    }}
  </div>
{%- endif -%}
